## Why This Is Asked

Explores a practical, new angle on CBA: on-device prefetching to cut latency, balancing storage, energy, privacy, and business impact. Tests the ability to translate UX improvements into measurable costs and decision criteria.

## Key Concepts

- On-device predictor energy/compute
- Cache budgeting and invalidation strategies
- Data transfer vs. storage costs
- Privacy/opt-in and data minimization
- Latency impact and engagement metrics
- Break-even analysis and go/no-go thresholds

## Code Example

```javascript
function calcBreakeven(params){
  const {users, cacheMB, energyPerHit, savedMsPerHit, valuePerMs, pricePerMB} = params;
  const energyCost = energyPerHit * users;
  const dataValue = savedMsPerHit * users * valuePerMs;
  const breakevenMonths = (cacheMB * pricePerMB) / ((dataValue) - energyCost);
  return breakevenMonths;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you calibrate the model without A/B tests for small pilots?
- What privacy constraints would you enforce for on-device prefetching data?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a practical on-device data structure with incremental updates, offline-first UX, and a clear 12‑month cost plan, including privacy considerations.

## Key Concepts

- Local inverted index and incremental updates
- Offline-first data paths and cache invalidation
- Delta-based cloud sync and conflict resolution
- Resource budgeting: storage, CPU, memory, latency, energy
- Privacy, compliance, and user consent handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal incremental inverted index (conceptual)
class InvertedIndex {
  constructor() {
    this.index = {}; // term -> Set of note IDs
  }
  addNote(id, text) {
    text.split(/\W+/).forEach(t => {
      if (!t) return;
      const w = t.toLowerCase();
      (this.index[w] ||= new Set()).add(id);
    });
  }
  search(q) {
    return q
      .toLowerCase()
      .split(/\s+/)
      .flatMap(w => Array.from(this.index[w] || []));
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you estimate index maintenance energy and storage growth over time?
- How would you handle search latency guarantees on mid-range devices with thousands of notes?
- What privacy safeguards would you implement for on-device indexing and sync deltas?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of edge compute architecture, privacy-preserving data transfer, and cross-region cost modeling. It forces candidates to make trade-offs between latency, bandwidth, and regulatory compliance while demonstrating practical planning skills.

## Key Concepts

- **Edge locality and regional caching**: Evaluating feature flags at the CDN edge minimizes round-trip time to origin servers
- **Privacy-preserving deltas**: Shipping only anonymized changes rather than full state reduces bandwidth and exposure risk
- **Data residency and encryption**: Compliance with GDPR, CCPA, and regional data sovereignty requirements adds infrastructure cost
- **Phased rollout**: Gradual deployment with canary releases reduces risk across regions
- **Drift detection and rollback**: Monitoring for configuration drift and automated rollback procedures ensure reliability
- **Break-even analysis**: Quantifying backend load reduction, latency gains, and operational savings against implementation costs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal CBA data model sketch
class RegionCost {
  constructor(region, cacheKB, latencyMs, deltaKB) {
    this.region = region;
    this.cacheKB = cacheKB;
    this.latencyMs = latencyMs;
    this.deltaKB = deltaKB;
  }

  monthlyBandwidth(deltaKB, requestsPerMonth) {
    return (deltaKB * requestsPerMonth) / 1024; // MB/month
  }

  annualCost(monthlyBandwidthMB, complianceTier) {
    const bandwidthCost = monthlyBandwidthMB * 12 * 0.05; // $0.05/MB
    const complianceCost = complianceTier * 5000; // $5k-$15k/region
    return bandwidthCost + complianceCost;
  }
}

const regions = [
  new RegionCost('us-east', 32, 1.2, 0.5),
  new RegionCost('eu-west', 24, 1.8, 0.4),
  new RegionCost('ap-south', 16, 2.5, 0.3)
];
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
This evaluates cost modeling for federated, multi-app ML with privacy constraints—a realistic production use case at companies like Google (Gboard), Apple, and Meta. It tests ability to identify cost drivers, trade-offs between privacy and accuracy, and phased rollout planning under regulatory constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Federated learning with secure aggregation and differential privacy
- Cross-app governance, regional data locality (GDPR, CCPA, PIPL)
- Bandwidth, energy, and memory budgeting on mobile devices
- Model drift detection, concept drift, and maintenance pipelines
- Phased regional rollout with go/no-go gates

## Code Example
```javascript
function calculateCBAPhase(devices, roundsPerDay, daysPerMonth, payloadKB, costPerGB) {
  const activeDevices = devices * 0.30;
  const monthlyUplinkGB = activeDevices * roundsPerDay * daysPerMonth * payloadKB / 1024 / 1024;
  const monthlyCost = monthlyUplinkGB * costPerGB;
  return {
    devices, activeDevices, monthlyUplinkGB, monthlyCost,
    breakEvenUsers: monthlyCost / 0.10
  };
}

function rolloutPhase(phase, criteria) {
  return {
    phase,
    go: criteria.every(c => c.passed),
    gates: criteria
  };
}
```

This question distinguishes senior ML engineers who can think end-to-end—from device constraints to business metrics.
